/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 73 0 R
/E (Cryptocurrency is a type of digital or virtual currency that is created and transferred electronically. Unlike traditional currencies such as the Indian Rupee, US Dollar, or Euro, cryptocurrency is generally not issued or controlled by a central bank. It uses cryptographic techniques to secure transactions and control the creation of new units. The basic idea behind cryptocurrency is to allow people to transfer value directly through a digital network without always depending on a traditional financial institution as an intermediary. Cryptocurrency transactions are recorded on a blockchain, which is a digital ledger maintained by a distributed network of computers. The first widely known cryptocurrency was Bitcoin, introduced in 2009. Since then, thousands of cryptocurrencies and digital tokens have been developed for different purposes. Some are designed mainly as digital currencies, while others support applications such as smart contracts, decentralized finance, gaming, and digital ownership. Cryptocurrency has become an important topic in modern finance and technology because it combines economics, computer science, cryptography, and networking. However, cryptocurrencies also involve significant risks, including price volatility, scams, hacking, and regulatory uncertainty. Therefore, understanding how cryptocurrency works is important before discussing its advantages, disadvantages, and possible future applications.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
