95 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (The development of cryptocurrency is connected to earlier attempts to create digital forms of money. Before Bitcoin, several digital-cash projects attempted to solve problems related to online payments, but many struggled with issues such as trust, security, and the need for a central authority. In 2008, a person or group using the name Satoshi Nakamoto published the Bitcoin white paper, describing a peer-to-peer electronic cash system. Bitcoin was launched in 2009 and introduced a system in which transactions could be recorded on a distributed ledger without requiring a traditional central authority to maintain it. Bitcoin's success encouraged developers to create other blockchain-based projects. Ethereum, launched in 2015, expanded the idea by introducing a platform capable of running smart contracts and decentralized applications. After Ethereum, many other blockchain networks and tokens were developed, each offering different features and purposes. Over time, cryptocurrency moved from being a relatively specialized technological experiment to becoming a major subject of discussion in finance, technology, business, and regulation. The cryptocurrency ecosystem has continued to develop through innovations such as stablecoins, decentralized finance, non-fungible tokens, and blockchain-based applications. This history shows how digital currencies have evolved from the idea of online electronic cash into a much broader technological ecosystem.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
